{{!--
  Spielplan: the Jellyfin Webhook plugin template for §7.2's `POST /events/jellyfin`.
  Published the way `.env.example` publishes config, and for the same reason: this file is the
  contract (decision 365). The app cannot see the plugin's configuration, so the shape it
  tolerates has to be written somewhere an operator can paste rather than only in its tests.

  WHERE IT GOES. Jellyfin -> Dashboard -> Plugins -> Webhook -> Add Generic Destination.
    Webhook Url            <PUBLIC_URL>/events/jellyfin
    Notification Type      Item Added        (this template is for that one event)
    Item Type              tick Movies, Episodes, Series; UNTICK Season, Albums, Songs, Videos
    Template               everything below this comment
    Add Request Header     X-Spielplan-Token : <the token the Jellyfin card showed you once>

  THE PLUGIN TICKS ALL SEVEN ITEM TYPES ON A DESTINATION IT HAS JUST ADDED, so the four this app
  does not acquire stay ticked until you untick them. Left ticked, nothing breaks - each of their
  adds is answered 202 and recorded like any other - but every season of a new show and every
  track of an album is then a delivery of its own, and each one costs a row, a read of your server
  inside the sweep's time budget and a skipped row whose reason reads as a fault when there is
  none. A large music import can hold a film's add behind it until the delta poll files the film.

  THE APP REQUIRES TWO FIELDS AND TREATS THE REST AS ADVISORY: `ItemId` and `ItemType`, which
  are the whole of what the debounce can act on. A delivery missing either is still accepted
  (202) and recorded with that reason rather than refused, because a webhook the app rejects is
  one you never learn about - but it enqueues nothing, so a template that drops them is a
  webhook that silently does nothing at all.

  `SeriesId` IS WHAT MAKES A SEASON ONE JOB. An Episode is never acquired as itself: the app
  resolves it through its series and files one job for the show (§7.2, "series acquire
  per-show, not per-episode"; decision 369). An Episode delivery with no `SeriesId` is recorded
  and enqueues nothing - it is never guessed at - so dropping that line turns a twelve-episode
  scan into twelve refusals.

  `NotificationType` lets one URL carry several of the plugin's templates: anything other than
  `ItemAdded` is recorded and not acted on. It may be omitted, and is then read as `ItemAdded`.
  `Name`, `SeriesName` and `Year` are for the operator reading §6.6's card, never for matching.

  WHY THE TWO NAMES HAVE THREE BRACES. The plugin puts nothing between a title and this JSON
  that makes the title JSON. It backslash-escapes each double quote in `Name` and `SeriesName`
  before Handlebars runs, and a two-brace `{{Name}}` is then HTML-encoded - so a title with a
  quote in it rendered `\&quot;`, which no JSON parser reads, and "Law & Order" arrived as
  `Law &amp; Order`. Three braces write the escaped title as it is, which is a legal JSON string
  for any title without a backslash or a control character in it (decision 417). Nothing on the
  plugin Jellyfin 10.9 and 10.10 run can write those two as JSON: `json_encode` first shipped in
  plugin 18, for 10.11, and on an older plugin a helper it does not know throws and nothing at
  all is delivered - so do not add one unless every server you point here runs plugin 18 or
  later. A title it cannot carry still arrives, is answered 202 and recorded with its reason
  rather than refused into a plugin that does not retry (decision 365), and the delta poll files
  a new film or show at its next run all the same.

  WHY THE FIELDS ARE QUOTED, AND WHY THEY ARE IN THIS ORDER. The four fields the app acts on -
  `NotificationType`, `ItemId`, `ItemType`, `SeriesId` - are emitted LAST, after every free-text
  one. A name that closed its own string and opened a second `"ItemId"` would be a legal object,
  and a parser keeps the LAST of two duplicate keys; the plugin's quote escaping is what stops a
  title doing that today, and it is the plugin's detail rather than this app's, so the order is
  what keeps a forged duplicate inert if a plugin build ever stops escaping (decision 405). Keep
  that order if you edit this file, and keep every value quoted: it also stops a numeric field
  from becoming a syntax error of its own.
--}}
{
  "Name": "{{{Name}}}",
  "SeriesName": "{{{SeriesName}}}",
  "Year": "{{Year}}",
  "NotificationType": "{{NotificationType}}",
  "ItemId": "{{ItemId}}",
  "ItemType": "{{ItemType}}",
  "SeriesId": "{{SeriesId}}"
}
